node_modules

# Local env files. `frontend/.env` is the only file the Vite build actually
# reads, so keeping it out of version control is what stops a stale value from
# sneaking into a deploy. `.env.example` is the tracked template. The root
# .gitignore also filters `.env*`, but keeping the entries here too is
# defence-in-depth: a copy of the frontend repo alone still ignores secrets.
.env
.env.local
.env.development.local
.env.test.local
.env.production.local

# `dist/` is the built frontend output. It is a build artifact, not source,
# so it must not be tracked. Netlify builds from source on every deploy
# (`netlify.toml` runs `npm run build` and publishes `dist`), and
# `git status` is much quieter when the minified bundle isn't part of every
# PR diff.
#
# Note: the previous version of this file explicitly excluded `dist` from
# the ignore rule, on the grounds that PythonAnywhere had no Node runtime
# and needed the pre-built bundle in the repo. Production actually runs on
# Netlify, which builds from source, so that workaround is stale. The
# `!frontend/dist/` exception in the root .gitignore has been removed to
# match. See the 2026-10 audit, finding M6.
dist/
dist-ssr/

